sadness and anger at the vigil in the  murder favela one of the largest slums  of rio de janeiro  residents gathered on friday one day  after a police raid left 19 dead  they protested against the violence they  say they've been facing for decades  violence is a product of government  policies which have left the poor with  no jobs no schools no food on the table  if a young boy is holding a rifle now  that's because in 2010 when he was a  child he was given no opportunities  four hundred policemen surrounded the  moldova on thursday new video shows them  shooting down a man  the police say they were targeting  leaders of the red commando one of  brazil's largest drug gangs but met  resistance  the criminal leaders ordered their men  to resist arrest or face retaliation  16 suspects one policeman and two  innocent bystanders were killed spurring  public outrage  three of the four most lethal police  raids in the history of rio de janeiro  happened during the past  14 months  more than 70 people were killed  including those who died here in the  mojo duale mount favela  president  has long favored heavy-handed police  tactics  but human rights organizations and crime  experts say violence has proven to be  ineffective  all these deaths have not stopped  criminal gangs from growing stronger so  why insist on lethal operations instead  of concentrating on investigation and  intelligence gathering that's what will  prevent drug lords from getting armed  at the vigil on friday residents of the  favela prayed for peace and the future  of their children  monika yanakev al-jazeera rio de janeiro
 

